========== 1. JF-1 — what the org CLI Design Standard requires of a post-result failure ==========
org CLI Design Standard v1.4.14, blob f9de0437baece3383e73ca2c5f48b7ab399b51c7, 77990 bytes

--- R7.8 — the envelope is required even when output has already been produced ---
[R01 PASS] R7.8 requires the single stderr error object INCLUDING a failure that occurs mid-stream — a failure after output has begun still gets the envelope
[R02 PASS] R7.8 nowhere requires stdout to be empty on an error path — the envelope rule governs stderr only

--- R7.1 — the stream split that decides where each half goes ---
[R03 PASS] R7.1 exists
[R04 PASS] R7.1: stdout carries the REQUESTED RESULT and stderr everything else — exactly the two halves a post-result clipboard failure produces
[R04b PASS] R7.1 contemplates a diagnostic raised DURING a normal data command and routes it to stderr — it does not withdraw the data already written to stdout

--- R6.3 — read at its literal scope, not stretched ---
[R05 PASS] R6.3 exists and is a SHOULD
[R06 PASS] R6.3's literal scope is a command operating on MANY ITEMS. It is a shape analogy for --copy, NOT a rule that binds it; the binding exit-code rule is R8.2. Recording this stops the decision from citing R6.3 more absolutely than it reads.

--- R8.2 — the binding exit-status assignment ---
[R07 PASS] R8.2 assigns exit 1 to runtime failures — not the exit-2 arm, which is for required input or consent that prompting cannot supply


